1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,866
Welcome. This video presents TaskFlow, a
project and task management web application.

2
00:00:05,906 --> 00:00:09,110
In the first part, we look at the
technology stack,

3
00:00:09,150 --> 00:00:12,608
with the exact versions and real code from
the project.

4
00:00:12,648 --> 00:00:17,188
In the second part, we use the running
application from start to finish.

5
00:00:19,328 --> 00:00:21,730
TaskFlow is built in three layers.

6
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:25,249
The browser runs a React single-page
application.

7
00:00:25,289 --> 00:00:28,624
Its built files are served by nginx, which
also

8
00:00:28,664 --> 00:00:32,143
forwards every request under /api to the
backend.

9
00:00:32,183 --> 00:00:35,375
The backend is a Spring Boot REST API
written

10
00:00:35,415 --> 00:00:38,607
in Java, and it keeps its data in
PostgreSQL.

11
00:00:38,647 --> 00:00:42,629
Flyway keeps the database schema up to
date, e-mail goes

12
00:00:42,669 --> 00:00:47,584
through a mail service, and the whole
stack runs with Docker Compose.

13
00:00:49,224 --> 00:00:52,128
The user interface is built with React,

14
00:00:52,168 --> 00:00:55,676
version 19.2.8, and React Router, version
7.18.

15
00:00:55,716 --> 00:00:58,469
Here is the route table from App.jsx.

16
00:00:58,509 --> 00:01:01,941
The sign-in and registration pages are
public.

17
00:01:01,981 --> 00:01:07,300
Every other page sits inside
ProtectedRoute, so it needs a session, and

18
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:12,584
it is drawn inside the shared layout with
the sidebar and the top bar.

19
00:01:14,224 --> 00:01:19,504
The frontend is written in plain
JavaScript with JSX, as ES modules.

20
00:01:19,544 --> 00:01:25,059
Every screen talks to the REST API through
one helper, called apiFetch.

21
00:01:25,099 --> 00:01:31,396
It sends JSON to a path under /api, and
adds the access token as a bearer header.

22
00:01:31,436 --> 00:01:34,526
If the token has expired, the request is

23
00:01:34,566 --> 00:01:37,968
retried once after the session is
refreshed.

24
00:01:39,608 --> 00:01:42,861
Styling uses Tailwind CSS, version 4.3.3.

25
00:01:42,901 --> 00:01:46,797
In Tailwind four, the configuration lives
in CSS.

26
00:01:46,837 --> 00:01:51,215
Each token inside the theme block, such as
the charcoal

27
00:01:51,255 --> 00:01:55,713
colour, becomes a utility class that the
components use.

28
00:01:55,753 --> 00:01:59,247
The dark theme redefines the same
variables.

29
00:01:59,287 --> 00:02:01,576
Icons come from Lucide React.

30
00:02:03,216 --> 00:02:06,438
Vite, version 8.2.2, is the build tool,
and

31
00:02:06,478 --> 00:02:10,154
npm installs the dependencies from the
lock file.

32
00:02:10,194 --> 00:02:14,705
The Vite configuration loads the Tailwind
and React plugins.

33
00:02:14,745 --> 00:02:19,863
During development, requests to /api are
forwarded to the backend on

34
00:02:19,903 --> 00:02:25,400
port 8080, so the frontend uses the same
relative paths as in production.

35
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:30,069
Charts are drawn with Recharts, version
3.10.

36
00:02:30,109 --> 00:02:34,025
This is the team productivity chart from
the Reports page.

37
00:02:34,065 --> 00:02:37,912
It is a bar chart of the tasks each member
has completed,

38
00:02:37,952 --> 00:02:41,663
and its colours come from the same CSS
variables as the

39
00:02:41,703 --> 00:02:45,960
rest of the interface, so it follows the
light and dark themes.

40
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:51,684
The backend is written in Java 25, on
Spring Boot 4.0.8,

41
00:02:51,724 --> 00:02:56,251
which brings Spring Framework 7 and an
embedded Tomcat server.

42
00:02:56,291 --> 00:02:59,639
Controllers map HTTP requests to Java
methods.

43
00:02:59,679 --> 00:03:05,236
These two endpoints let a user accept or
decline an invitation to a project.

44
00:03:05,276 --> 00:03:11,128
The service behind them checks that the
invitation really belongs to the caller.

45
00:03:12,768 --> 00:03:17,295
Database access uses Spring Data JPA with
Hibernate, version 7.2.

46
00:03:17,335 --> 00:03:22,002
A repository is an interface, and Spring
writes the code behind it.

47
00:03:22,042 --> 00:03:26,358
This query returns the projects a user is
an active member of.

48
00:03:26,398 --> 00:03:30,924
It is the visibility boundary of the whole
application: a pending

49
00:03:30,964 --> 00:03:36,896
invitation does not count, so it never shows a
project to someone who has not joined.

50
00:03:38,536 --> 00:03:40,779
The data lives in PostgreSQL 18.

51
00:03:40,819 --> 00:03:44,418
Some rules are enforced inside the
database itself.

52
00:03:44,458 --> 00:03:47,914
This trigger function refuses to assign a
task to

53
00:03:47,954 --> 00:03:51,980
anyone who is not an active member of the
task's project.

54
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:56,832
So the rule holds, even for a request that
bypasses the application.

55
00:03:58,472 --> 00:04:01,897
Schema changes are handled by Flyway,
version 11.

56
00:04:01,937 --> 00:04:07,484
In Docker Compose, a one-shot migrate
container runs before the backend starts.

57
00:04:07,524 --> 00:04:11,515
It applies every versioned SQL file that
the database has

58
00:04:11,555 --> 00:04:15,688
not seen yet, so an existing database is
upgraded in place.

59
00:04:17,328 --> 00:04:21,357
Authentication is handled by Spring
Security, version 7.

60
00:04:21,397 --> 00:04:23,392
The server keeps no session.

61
00:04:23,432 --> 00:04:27,461
Apart from a short list of public paths,
such as sign-in

62
00:04:27,501 --> 00:04:31,458
and registration, every request must carry
a valid JWT.

63
00:04:31,498 --> 00:04:34,727
The OAuth 2 resource server checks the
token,

64
00:04:34,767 --> 00:04:38,288
and turns it into the caller's identity
and role.

65
00:04:39,928 --> 00:04:44,985
Access tokens are created with the JJWT
library, version 0.12.6.

66
00:04:45,025 --> 00:04:49,127
Each token carries the username, when it
was issued,

67
00:04:49,167 --> 00:04:53,666
when it expires, and the system role if
the user has one.

68
00:04:53,706 --> 00:04:57,012
It is signed with the server's secret key.

69
00:04:57,052 --> 00:05:01,392
A separate refresh token is kept in an
HttpOnly cookie.

70
00:05:03,032 --> 00:05:04,955
Authorization is role based.

71
00:05:04,995 --> 00:05:08,741
Seven permissions, view, create, edit,
delete, assign,

72
00:05:08,781 --> 00:05:12,527
approve and generate reports, are granted
per role and

73
00:05:12,567 --> 00:05:16,312
per resource, at system level and inside
each project.

74
00:05:16,352 --> 00:05:19,537
These rows are the defaults for a Team
Member.

75
00:05:19,577 --> 00:05:24,304
They may view the project, change a task's
status, and add subtasks,

76
00:05:24,344 --> 00:05:29,492
comments, time and files, but they cannot
create tasks or delete anything.

77
00:05:29,532 --> 00:05:33,208
The Administrator can change these grants
in the app.

78
00:05:34,848 --> 00:05:37,986
E-mail is sent with Spring Boot's mail
starter.

79
00:05:38,026 --> 00:05:41,434
Locally it goes to Mailpit, a test mail
server, and

80
00:05:41,474 --> 00:05:44,815
the hosted version can send through the
Gmail API.

81
00:05:44,855 --> 00:05:47,316
One-time codes are six random digits.

82
00:05:47,356 --> 00:05:50,089
Only a bcrypt hash of the code is stored,

83
00:05:50,129 --> 00:05:53,672
together with an expiry time and a limit
on attempts.

84
00:05:55,312 --> 00:05:57,722
Reports are exported in the browser.

85
00:05:57,762 --> 00:06:00,784
write-excel-file creates the spreadsheet,
and

86
00:06:00,824 --> 00:06:04,119
jsPDF with its auto table plugin creates
the PDF.

87
00:06:04,159 --> 00:06:07,793
Both libraries are imported only when
someone asks for

88
00:06:07,833 --> 00:06:11,400
a file, so they do not slow down the first
page load.

89
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,977
The backend is built with Maven, and both

90
00:06:16,017 --> 00:06:19,825
halves ship as Docker images with
multi-stage builds.

91
00:06:19,865 --> 00:06:23,746
The first stage compiles the jar with
Maven on JDK 25.

92
00:06:23,786 --> 00:06:27,304
The second stage copies only the jar into
a small

93
00:06:27,344 --> 00:06:31,298
Java runtime image, which runs as an
unprivileged user.

94
00:06:31,338 --> 00:06:35,800
The frontend image is built the same way,
and served by nginx.

95
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,523
Testing happens at three levels.

96
00:06:39,563 --> 00:06:44,631
Vitest runs the frontend unit tests, JUnit
with the Spring Boot test starters

97
00:06:44,671 --> 00:06:50,138
covers the backend, and Playwright drives
a real browser against the running stack.

98
00:06:50,178 --> 00:06:53,588
This test registers a new person, reads
the e-mailed

99
00:06:53,628 --> 00:06:57,104
code from Mailpit, verifies the account
and signs in.

100
00:06:58,744 --> 00:07:02,550
That is the stack. Now let's open the
running application, and

101
00:07:02,590 --> 00:07:05,280
follow one project from sign-up to
approval.

102
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:10,584
This is the home page, the first thing a
visitor sees.

103
00:07:10,624 --> 00:07:16,071
It explains what TaskFlow does: projects
and milestones, tasks with subtasks and

104
00:07:16,111 --> 00:07:21,832
checklists, a board and a calendar, roles
and approvals, notifications, and reports.

105
00:07:22,785 --> 00:07:27,258
Let's create an account. We choose a
username and an e-mail address.

106
00:07:27,298 --> 00:07:29,779
As we type the password, the checklist

107
00:07:29,819 --> 00:07:32,633
underneath turns green, one rule at a
time.

108
00:07:33,586 --> 00:07:35,959
If the confirmation does not match the

109
00:07:35,999 --> 00:07:38,754
password, the form refuses it, and says
why.

110
00:07:39,700 --> 00:07:42,804
We correct the confirmation, and create
the account.

111
00:07:43,753 --> 00:07:48,696
TaskFlow e-mails a six-digit code, to
prove that the address is real.

112
00:07:48,736 --> 00:07:51,441
We type the code in, and press Verify.

113
00:07:52,393 --> 00:07:57,513
The account is verified, and it starts as
a regular user. Let's sign in with it.

114
00:08:00,025 --> 00:08:02,669
The dashboard shows only zeros, because

115
00:08:02,709 --> 00:08:05,834
nobody has added this person to a project
yet.

116
00:08:05,874 --> 00:08:10,857
In TaskFlow, people only see the projects
they belong to. Let's sign out.

117
00:08:11,798 --> 00:08:14,357
Next, Olivia, a Project Manager.

118
00:08:14,397 --> 00:08:19,798
First, a wrong password: TaskFlow refuses
it, with a clear message.

119
00:08:20,745 --> 00:08:23,777
With the right password, Olivia is signed
in.

120
00:08:24,725 --> 00:08:27,719
Her dashboard sums up everything she can
see.

121
00:08:27,759 --> 00:08:32,507
The cards at the top count her projects,
active, completed and delayed,

122
00:08:32,547 --> 00:08:37,429
the average completion, and her tasks,
with how many are done or overdue.

123
00:08:38,369 --> 00:08:42,019
Below, the task overview breaks the work
down by status,

124
00:08:42,059 --> 00:08:46,104
and the due and overdue list puts the most
urgent tasks first.

125
00:08:46,144 --> 00:08:48,937
On the right, delayed projects are
flagged.

126
00:08:49,882 --> 00:08:53,986
The sidebar leads to every module: tasks,
projects, the

127
00:08:54,026 --> 00:08:58,506
calendar, the Kanban board, the team
directory, and reports.

128
00:08:59,452 --> 00:09:04,363
On the Projects page, each card shows a
project's status, progress and team.

129
00:09:04,403 --> 00:09:08,011
By default, only Administrators and
Project Managers can

130
00:09:08,051 --> 00:09:11,724
create projects, so only they see the New
Project button.

131
00:09:12,678 --> 00:09:18,251
Olivia enters a name, a priority, a start
and an end date, and a short description.

132
00:09:18,291 --> 00:09:20,618
TaskFlow gives the project its code

133
00:09:20,658 --> 00:09:24,878
automatically, and whoever creates a
project becomes its Owner.

134
00:09:25,833 --> 00:09:30,822
Every project has its own page, with its
code and manager at the top, then

135
00:09:30,862 --> 00:09:35,851
its status, priority, dates, and a
progress bar calculated from its tasks.

136
00:09:35,891 --> 00:09:39,521
Olivia moves the project from Planning to
In Progress.

137
00:09:40,466 --> 00:09:42,234
Next, she builds the team.

138
00:09:42,274 --> 00:09:45,571
She opens Add member, gives Owen the Team
Leader

139
00:09:45,611 --> 00:09:49,186
role, shown here as Admin, and sends the
invitation.

140
00:09:50,134 --> 00:09:53,227
Mia is invited as a Team Member in the
same way.

141
00:09:53,267 --> 00:09:56,034
Both are now listed as pending
invitations.

142
00:09:56,074 --> 00:09:58,710
Nobody joins a project until they accept.

143
00:09:59,653 --> 00:10:02,134
Projects are planned with milestones.

144
00:10:02,174 --> 00:10:05,337
Olivia adds one called Launch, but its due
date

145
00:10:05,377 --> 00:10:08,949
falls after the project ends, so TaskFlow
refuses it.

146
00:10:09,900 --> 00:10:12,707
With a due date inside the project's
dates,

147
00:10:12,747 --> 00:10:15,620
the milestone is added, with its own
status.

148
00:10:16,562 --> 00:10:20,207
Now Owen signs in. His bell shows Olivia's
invitation.

149
00:10:20,247 --> 00:10:23,755
He accepts it, and joins the project as
Team Leader.

150
00:10:23,795 --> 00:10:25,666
He could also have declined.

151
00:10:29,592 --> 00:10:32,768
Mia does the same, and joins as a Team
Member.

152
00:10:42,485 --> 00:10:45,969
Back with Olivia, both now appear in the
members list.

153
00:10:46,009 --> 00:10:48,710
As the Owner, she makes Owen the project's

154
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:51,973
lead, and his name appears at the top of
the page.

155
00:11:00,694 --> 00:11:06,247
Now for the work itself. Olivia adds a
task: Build the one-page checkout form.

156
00:11:06,287 --> 00:11:09,115
She sets a high priority, a due date, an

157
00:11:09,155 --> 00:11:12,342
estimate of six hours, and assigns it to
Mia.

158
00:11:13,287 --> 00:11:17,759
She adds a second task, Launch the
checkout page, and opens it.

159
00:11:22,346 --> 00:11:25,953
In the task panel, she makes it depend on
the checkout form.

160
00:11:25,993 --> 00:11:29,722
Until the form is finished, the launch
task counts as blocked.

161
00:11:30,664 --> 00:11:32,845
Let's follow Mia, the Team Member.

162
00:11:32,885 --> 00:11:35,980
Her bell shows the task she has just been
given.

163
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:39,377
TaskFlow also sends reminders three days
and one day

164
00:11:39,417 --> 00:11:43,296
before a deadline, and a notice when a
task becomes overdue.

165
00:11:44,250 --> 00:11:46,891
The Tasks page lists every task in her

166
00:11:46,931 --> 00:11:50,348
projects, grouped by project, and then by
status.

167
00:11:50,388 --> 00:11:54,722
Quick views pick out today's, upcoming, in
progress, completed

168
00:11:54,762 --> 00:11:59,378
and overdue work, and there is a search
box, filters, and sorting.

169
00:12:00,324 --> 00:12:03,888
Mia opens her task, and the detail panel
slides in.

170
00:12:03,928 --> 00:12:06,644
She moves it from To Do to In Progress.

171
00:12:07,596 --> 00:12:12,488
She breaks the work into two subtasks, and
adds a checklist item for testing.

172
00:12:12,528 --> 00:12:15,050
Every change is saved straight away, and

173
00:12:15,090 --> 00:12:17,804
recorded in the project's activity
history.

174
00:12:18,747 --> 00:12:22,690
She attaches a wireframe, so everyone
works from the same design.

175
00:12:22,730 --> 00:12:25,203
Each file can be up to ten megabytes, and

176
00:12:25,243 --> 00:12:27,899
file types that are not allowed are
refused.

177
00:12:28,843 --> 00:12:32,276
Next, she logs two and a half hours, with
a short note.

178
00:12:32,316 --> 00:12:34,865
The bar compares the logged time with the

179
00:12:34,905 --> 00:12:37,770
six-hour estimate, and shows how much is
left.

180
00:12:37,810 --> 00:12:40,107
She could also start a timer instead.

181
00:12:41,052 --> 00:12:43,032
Then she leaves a comment for Owen.

182
00:12:43,072 --> 00:12:45,744
Typing the at sign lists the project's
members,

183
00:12:45,784 --> 00:12:48,572
and mentioning someone sends them a
notification.

184
00:12:49,522 --> 00:12:52,797
When the work is done, she ticks off the
subtasks and

185
00:12:52,837 --> 00:12:56,049
the checklist item, and submits the task
for review.

186
00:12:56,089 --> 00:12:58,864
A task cannot be completed while subtasks
are

187
00:12:58,904 --> 00:13:01,866
still open, and Mia cannot approve her own
work.

188
00:13:02,812 --> 00:13:06,590
The Kanban board shows the same tasks as
cards, in five

189
00:13:06,630 --> 00:13:10,963
columns: to do, in progress, in review,
blocked, and completed.

190
00:13:11,003 --> 00:13:15,476
Each card shows the project, the priority,
the time spent and the

191
00:13:15,516 --> 00:13:20,196
due date, and cards can be dragged, or
moved with their move button.

192
00:13:21,141 --> 00:13:23,674
What about a project Mia is not part of?

193
00:13:23,714 --> 00:13:26,054
TaskFlow simply says the page was not

194
00:13:26,094 --> 00:13:29,142
found, so nobody can learn which projects
exist.

195
00:13:29,182 --> 00:13:32,229
The same error page appears for any
address that

196
00:13:32,269 --> 00:13:35,381
does not exist, with a way back to the
dashboard.

197
00:13:36,329 --> 00:13:41,309
Now Owen signs in. His bell says a task is
waiting for his review, and the same

198
00:13:41,349 --> 00:13:45,313
request appears in a yellow block at the
top of his Tasks page.

199
00:13:53,526 --> 00:13:54,778
He opens the task.

200
00:13:54,818 --> 00:14:00,878
Requesting changes, or rejecting the work,
needs a comment, so Mia knows what to fix.

201
00:14:01,818 --> 00:14:03,646
This time, the work is good.

202
00:14:03,686 --> 00:14:06,115
Owen adds a comment, and approves it.

203
00:14:06,155 --> 00:14:07,850
The task is now completed.

204
00:14:08,801 --> 00:14:13,665
Back with Olivia. The project's progress
has updated by itself, the finished

205
00:14:13,705 --> 00:14:17,665
task is crossed out, and the launch task
is no longer blocked.

206
00:14:26,655 --> 00:14:29,134
For a fuller picture, let's open Website

207
00:14:29,174 --> 00:14:32,471
Redesign, a project that has been running
for months.

208
00:14:32,511 --> 00:14:35,431
Four views show its work from different
angles.

209
00:14:35,471 --> 00:14:39,839
Team tasks lists each member's tasks, and
lets managers reassign them.

210
00:14:40,791 --> 00:14:45,964
Workload compares each person's assigned,
active and overdue tasks, and their

211
00:14:46,004 --> 00:14:51,719
hours, with the team average, and marks them
as overloaded, balanced, or underloaded.

212
00:14:52,661 --> 00:14:55,889
The timeline places the project, its
milestones

213
00:14:55,929 --> 00:14:59,365
and its tasks on one time axis, with today
marked.

214
00:15:00,308 --> 00:15:03,656
The Gantt chart draws each task as a bar,
coloured

215
00:15:03,696 --> 00:15:07,180
by status, with the dependencies drawn
between them.

216
00:15:08,122 --> 00:15:13,697
The calendar shows task due dates and
milestones, by month, by week, or by day.

217
00:15:13,737 --> 00:15:19,098
Clicking an item opens its day in a side
drawer, with the details beside it.

218
00:15:20,044 --> 00:15:24,192
Reports turn the data into charts: task
counts, completion

219
00:15:24,232 --> 00:15:28,740
over time, team productivity, and the
progress of each project.

220
00:15:29,694 --> 00:15:33,350
The KPI tab calculates the completion
rates, the overdue

221
00:15:33,390 --> 00:15:37,046
rate, the on-time rate, and the average
completion time.

222
00:15:38,001 --> 00:15:40,545
There are also seven named reports.

223
00:15:40,585 --> 00:15:43,645
Olivia chooses Website Redesign, generates

224
00:15:43,685 --> 00:15:48,665
the project report, and exports it to
Excel. PDF works the same way.

225
00:15:49,616 --> 00:15:53,357
The Team page is a directory of everyone
in the workspace.

226
00:15:53,397 --> 00:15:57,660
Selecting a person shows their e-mail,
their assigned tasks, their

227
00:15:57,700 --> 00:16:02,224
position and department, and their
projects, with each one's progress.

228
00:16:03,164 --> 00:16:04,936
Finally, the Administrator.

229
00:16:04,976 --> 00:16:09,904
Because this account has the most power,
the password alone is not enough.

230
00:16:09,944 --> 00:16:12,992
TaskFlow e-mails a six-digit sign-in code,
and

231
00:16:13,032 --> 00:16:16,348
the session starts only once that code is
entered.

232
00:16:17,297 --> 00:16:22,309
On the Users page, the Administrator
manages every account: the system

233
00:16:22,349 --> 00:16:27,721
role, the personal details, and the
status, active, inactive, or suspended.

234
00:16:28,662 --> 00:16:31,924
Accounts can also be deleted, after a
confirmation.

235
00:16:31,964 --> 00:16:36,715
TaskFlow suggests making an account
inactive instead, to keep its history.

236
00:16:36,755 --> 00:16:40,406
Here, we delete the test account we
created at the start.

237
00:16:41,347 --> 00:16:45,766
Roles and Permissions shows what each role
may do, as a grid of seven

238
00:16:45,806 --> 00:16:50,483
permissions: one grid for the whole
system, and one for inside a project.

239
00:16:50,523 --> 00:16:54,490
The Administrator can change the grants,
and create new roles.

240
00:16:54,530 --> 00:16:57,075
The Administrator role itself is locked.

241
00:16:58,029 --> 00:17:01,613
The audit log is a read-only record of
sensitive changes,

242
00:17:01,653 --> 00:17:05,301
such as role and permission changes, and
deleted accounts.

243
00:17:05,341 --> 00:17:10,134
The deletion we just made is already at
the top, with who made it, and when.

244
00:17:10,174 --> 00:17:13,949
The log can be searched, and filtered by
action and by date.

245
00:17:14,897 --> 00:17:17,153
Every user has personal settings.

246
00:17:17,193 --> 00:17:20,424
They choose a light, a dark, or a system
theme,

247
00:17:20,464 --> 00:17:23,833
and decide whether to receive task
notifications.

248
00:17:24,785 --> 00:17:28,171
The Profile page holds their photo, and
their personal

249
00:17:28,211 --> 00:17:32,106
details, such as their name, e-mail,
position, and department.

250
00:17:32,146 --> 00:17:34,961
Further down, they can change their
password,

251
00:17:35,001 --> 00:17:37,753
with the same strength checklist as
sign-up.

252
00:17:41,412 --> 00:17:45,788
Help and Support answers the most common
questions, grouped into getting

253
00:17:45,828 --> 00:17:50,204
started, projects and the team, work and
approvals, and troubleshooting.

254
00:17:53,687 --> 00:17:58,738
That is TaskFlow: projects, tasks, teams,
approvals and reports, in one

255
00:17:58,778 --> 00:18:03,972
place, with every person seeing and doing
exactly what their role allows.

256
00:18:04,012 --> 00:18:06,051
For safety, sessions also end

257
00:18:06,091 --> 00:18:10,855
automatically after a period of
inactivity. Thank you for watching.
